  },
  "expect": {
    "assertions": [
      "A1"
    ],
    "resources": [
      {
        "resource_id": "wallet-decimal",
        "kind": "onchain_wallet"
      }
    ],
    "balances": {
      "wallet-decimal": [
        {
          "category": "token_balance_wei_bare_int",
          "canonical_hint": null,
          "amount": {
            "asset": "eth-wei",
            "amount": "115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129639935"
          }
        },
        {
          "category": "token_balance_wei_float_token",
          "canonical_hint": null,
          "amount": {
            "asset": "eth-wei-reported-as-float",
            "amount": "115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129639935.0"
          }
        },
        {
          "category": "precise_fraction",
          "canonical_hint": null,
          "amount": {
            "asset": "USDC-precise",
            "amount": "123456789012345678.987654321"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "compare": "cmp_same_asset",
    "notes": "The wire never carries the raw provider payload -- it is embedded in THIS fixture as an escaped JSON STRING (script.on.balances\\.read[0].do[0].provider_payload_json) specifically so the outer conformance-runner's own json parsing of THIS fixture file never touches the float tokens either. fake_adapter.py parses that string itself with json.loads(text, parse_float=Decimal) and formats every extracted value with format(d, 'f') per spec/money.md section 3 (never str(Decimal(...)), which reproduces exponent notation for small magnitudes). Three tokens, three shapes: a bare 78-digit JSON integer (Python's json module hands this straight to int(), arbitrary precision, never at risk even without parse_float -- included because a real wei balance is realistically integral); the SAME 78-digit magnitude written with a trailing '.0' so Python's scanner classifies it as a float token and parse_float actually engages (this is the one an IEEE-754 double provably cannot round-trip: python3 -c \"print(format(float('115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129639935.0'),'.90g'))\" prints a value one higher than the true magnitude); and a 27-significant-digit decimal fraction (float(...) truncates it to '123456789012345680', silently losing the last ~10 digits). A host, or an adapter implementation, that runs this payload through a naive json.loads with no parse_float override will instead see 1.157920892373162e+77 and 1.2345678901234568e+17 -- and per spec/money.md section 2, a JSON *number* in the amount field must be rejected outright at the deserializer, so even a 'faithful' re-stringification of that float would be doubly wrong: wrong value AND wrong wire shape (a Rust f64 formatted back to a string is still evidence the number touched a float somewhere in the pipeline, which is exactly what A1 is checking for)."
  }
}
